#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86137b90-ca62-515c-b8d1-fe28d477c1bf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b18540cc-5822-5620-a36f-60cfbbf3196b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4255" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 4}\\log_2 (x+4)$." :question/worked-solution "The function $\\log_2 (x+4)$ is defined for $x>-4$, so the limit as $x \\to 4$ exists.\n\nTo find this limit, we substitute $x=4$ into the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)\\log_{2} (x + 4) &= \\log_{2} (4 + 4) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (8) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (2^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f956a45f-29cb-5b78-a29f-3796e22af09d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Limits at Infinity of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "16cc98d7-5998-5727-a5c8-659e88dc158e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4257" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/f0/f00e550e8b543f64effcbafb43aed9e43a4363a0c3060da4fcc23d18f76a872f.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\log_2 \\left(x-1\\right) = \\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bae52078-17b6-536d-9cf8-4a92b811c2ae" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f8cafc9c-8e5b-5b95-bdc9-aedded6ebf52" :question/math-academy-id "e-4258" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x-2)\n$$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d107785c-82d2-5dbf-9293-e66fad67ba54" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating One-Sided Infinite Limits of Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "482f17ff-3008-5098-ad7e-18f1c72fb523" :question/math-academy-id "e-4259" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+2)\n$$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for\n\n$$\nx\\leq -2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty\n$$"}]}
